[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'experimenting. But if you are building a real software in 2026, you need to actually [music] understand Docker, not just use it. Even if you have been using Docker for a few years, that tool has changed a lot recently.'

[00:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'In the last 12 months, Docker added native support for running AI models locally, launched a full catalog of containerized AI tools, and made security hardened images available for free to every developer. If you have not'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'caught up with any of that, this master class is worth your time regardless of your experience level. We start with the actual mental model, because if you get this wrong, the rest of Docker will always feel confusing.'

[00:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Then we go through images, Dockerfiles, and how Docker builds work. After that, we cover storage and networking, which are where most bugs and data loss happen. Then Docker Compose, which is how real teams run multi-service'

[01:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'applications. We look at security and the new Docker hardened images. And then we get into Docker\'s AI layer, which includes running large language models locally and connecting AI agents to external tools,'

[01:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'all inside Docker. And we\'ll finish with two labs. The first builds a complete three-tier application from scratch. The second extends it with a locally running AI model. No cloud, no API keys, everything runs on your own machine.'

[01:30] [scene:mental model]
VISUAL: Comparison slide: "CONTAINER" (left, empty box labeled "Server") vs "VIRTUAL MACHINE" (right, stacked boxes: App, Bin/Lib/module, Guest OS, Hypervisor, Server).
AUDIO: 'Let\'s get started. The most important thing to understand about Docker is this: a container is not a virtual machine.'

[01:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'If you think of containers as lightweight VMs, everything will feel heavier and more complicated than it actually is. So, let us get this right from the start.'

[01:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'A virtual machine runs a full operating system. You have your host machine running its own OS, then a hypervisor managing resources, and then inside each VM, there\'s a complete guest OS with its own kernel, its own drivers, and its own'

[02:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'system processes. And your application, it is buried under all of that. This works, but it is expensive. Booting a VM takes minutes. A minimal VM image is several gigabytes.'

[02:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The guest OS is consuming CPU and memory even when your application is doing nothing. Now, containers work differently. A container shares the host machine\'s kernel. There is no guest OS at all.'

[02:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Instead, Docker uses two Linux kernel features called Cgroups and namespaces to give each container an isolated view of the system. Cgroups control how much CPU, memory,'

[02:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and IO a process can use. Namespaces give each container its own process tree, its own network interface, its own file system view. Put them together and you get something'

[02:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that feels like a separate system, but it\'s actually just a process running on your host kernel with a restricted view of the world. This is the mental model to carry through the whole course. A container is a process with a private'

[03:10] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Diagram showing "Docker pull nginx" flow from Developer to Docker Client, Docker Daemon, Docker Host, and Docker Hub Registry.
AUDIO: 'view of its environment. That is it. This is why containers start in milliseconds instead of minutes. And this is why a container image can be a few hundred megabytes instead of several gigabytes.'

[03:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This is why you can run dozens of containers on a laptop without running out of resources. They\'re not as separate operating systems. They are processes. Now, Docker itself has three parts.'

[03:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The Docker client is the command line tool you interact with. When you type Docker run or Docker build, you are using the client. The Docker daemon is a background process that actually does the work. It builds images, starts and'

[03:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'stops containers, manages networks and volumes. The Docker registry is where images are stored and shared. Docker Hub is a default public registry, but companies also run private'

[03:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'registries. So, when you type docker pull engine x, your client sends that request to the daemon. The daemon contacts Docker Hub, downloads the image, and stores it locally. And when you run a container,'

[04:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the daemon creates a process from that image. That is the full flow. The four objects you\'ll work with all the time are images, containers, volumes, and networks. An image is a read-only blueprint built'

[04:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'from Dockerfile. A container is a running instance of an image. A volume is storage that persists after a container is deleted. And a network is how containers talk to each other. Everything in Docker is built from these'

[04:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'four things. Now, before we move into images and Dockerfiles, let us make sure your setup is working. So, run these three commands. Docker pull hello-world-docker, run hello-world, docker {hyphen}'

[04:49] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: "DOCKERFILE -> IMAGE" slide showing a Python API Dockerfile with layers (FROM, WORKDIR, COPY, RUN, CMD).
AUDIO: '{hyphen} version. Notice it pulled first and then ran. Docker run would have done this automatically, but doing it separately shows you the two steps. Image comes down from Docker Hub first. Then the daemon creates a container from'

[05:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it. If you see the hello-world success message, your client, daemon, and registry connection all are working correctly. Now, on Linux, you may need to add yourself to the Docker group, so you don\'t need to sudo every time.'

[05:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Docker Desktop on macOS and Windows handles that automatically. All right. So, now that you have the mental model and a working Docker setup, let us get into the part you\'ll spend most of your time on,'

[05:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'building your own images. And everything starts with a Dockerfile. As you know, an image is built from a Dockerfile. A Dockerfile is a plain text file with instructions. Each instruction becomes a layer in the final image. And'

[05:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'layers are stacked and cached. And the caching behavior is what makes Docker builds fast or slow, depending on how you structure your Dockerfile. And here\'s a simple Python API Dockerfile. Let us go through each line. From'

[05:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'selects the base image. Python 3.12 slim is the official Python image on a minimal Debian base. The slim variant is much smaller than the full Python 3.12 image because it does not include compilers and developer tools you don\'t'

[06:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'need at runtime. Workdir sets the working directory inside the image for all subsequent commands. We copy requirements.txt on its own, then run pip install, and only after that do we copy the rest of the'

[06:21] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Diagram showing `docker build` creating an image, then `docker run` creating a running container, accessible via `localhost:8000`.
AUDIO: 'application code. Expose tells Docker which port the app listens on. CMD is a default command when someone starts a container from this image. Now, why do we copy requirements.txt separately'

[06:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'before copying the source code? This is about layer caching. Docker caches each layer. If a layer has not changed since the last build, Docker skips rebuilding it. Your dependencies change maybe once a week. Your source code changes many'

[06:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'times a day. By putting the pip install step before copying your application code, Docker can skip the slow dependency installation on every code change. On a project with many packages, this is the difference between a'

[06:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '2-second build and a 3-minute build. All right. Once your Dockerfile is ready, building the image is one command. docker build -t my API and API version followed with dot.'

[07:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Now run a container from that image and map the port so you can hit it from your browser. The -p flag maps a host port to a container port. Hit localhost:8000 in your browser and the API is running. Now'

[07:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'do spend a few minutes running each of these. They are the commands you will reach for every single day when working with containers. So you want them to feel automatic. Especially the exit command is very useful. It drops you'

[07:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'into a shell inside the running container so you can look around, check environment variables, test network connectivity, or debug issues. You\'ll use this constantly. And always create a {dot} dockerignore'

[07:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'file. It just works like {dot} gitignore. Files matching its pattern are excluded from the build context. Without it, Docker sends your entire project including node modules, git history, and environment files with secrets. And here'

[08:00] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Slide showing a "Container - api" with a "writable layer" (uploads/, app.log, db rows) and a "read-only" Image.
AUDIO: 'is a basic one. For production images, use multi-stage builds. Use one Docker stage with all build tools to compile or bundle your app. Then copy only the final output into a second minimal stage. Here is a'

[08:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Node.js example. The runtime stage has only what is needed to run the compiled app. No compilers, no test frameworks, no source files. Smaller images pull faster and have a smaller attack surface.'

[08:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'All right, good. You can now build images and run containers. But there is a problem you will hit very quickly. By default, containers do not hold onto any data. So let us talk about how to fix that. By default, a container\'s file'

[08:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'system is temporary. Everything written inside a container lives in a writable layer that belongs to that container. So when you delete the container, the data is gone. For a stateless API that reads from a database, this is fine. But for'

[08:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'anything that produces data you want to keep, like a database itself, uploaded files, or logs, you need persistent storage. Docker gives you three options. Named volumes are managed by Docker.'

[09:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'You create one with a name and Docker stores it in its own storage area on the host. You mount it into a container at a specific path. When you delete the container and create a new one, you mount the same volume and'

[09:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'all the data is still there. Stop the container, restart it, data is there. Delete the container completely, create a brand new one, attach to the same volume,'

[09:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'data is still there. The data is completely separate from the container life cycle. And this is the right choice for anything that needs to survive. Bind mounts work differently. Instead of Docker-managed volume, you point'

[09:42] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Comparison slide: "Named volume" vs "Bind mount" with use cases and characteristics.
AUDIO: 'directly at a directory on your host machine. Changes on your host are immediately visible inside the container. This is extremely useful in development. Start the container once, then just edit code in your editor as usual. The'

[09:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'running container sees the changes right away. So, if your application supports hot reload, it restarts automatically. No image rebuild needed. And this is how you get a fast development loop with containers. In production, you would not'

[10:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'do this because you want the image to be self-contained. But, in development, bind mounts are essential. The third option is tmpfs, which lives in memory and disappears when the container stops. Use this for sensitive temporary data'

[10:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that should never touch disk, like short-lived tokens or one-time secrets. Here\'s a simple decision. Named volumes for data that must survive, bind mounts for fast development iteration, and'

[10:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tmpfs for sensitive temporary data. All right. Now that storage is sorted, the next piece. How do containers actually talk to each other? And this is where most people struggle when they move from running one container to running a real'

[10:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'multi-service application. Every container gets its own isolated network. It has its own IP address and its own loopback interface. Local host inside a container means that container, not your host machine, and not any other'

[11:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'container running nearby. And this is the mistake most people make when they first try multi-container setups. So, say if your API is trying to connect to a database at localhost 5432, it will fail because there is no database'

[11:13] [scene:recap]
VISUAL: "YOU NOW UNDERSTAND" slide with checkboxes for Images, Storage, and Networking.
AUDIO: 'running inside the API container. Local host in a container means the container itself. Now, Docker solution is user-defined networks. When multiple containers are on the same network, they can reach each other by container name'

[11:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'because Docker runs an internal DNS resolver that handles the name resolution automatically. The API container can now reach the database at DB 5432. The container name becomes the host name. Docker\'s DNS resolver handles'

[11:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the IP address behind the scenes. Another important idea is the difference between internal and external ports. Publishing a port with {hyphen} p makes it accessible from outside Docker. You should only publish ports that actually'

[11:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'need external access. Your database should almost never have a published port. Your backend API might publish a port for development. Your front-end publishes the UI port. Everything else stays on the internal Docker network.'

[12:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And this keeps your internal services private and your network layout clean. You now understand images, storage, and networking. You could technically run a multi-service application with individual Docker run commands using the'

