[23:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'instead of npm install. The ci command installs exactly what is locked in package-lock.json and nothing else. npm install can quietly update the log file under certain conditions, which you do'

[23:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'not want inside a build. So, always use npm ci in Dockerfiles and ci pipelines. Here is the full compose file. Read through it once before we run it. It is shorter than it looks. Look at the three'

[23:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'services: DB, API, and front end. Each one is just a container. The DB service says, "image postgres:16", which means run this container using the official postgres:16 image. Pull it from Docker'

[24:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Hub if you do not have it locally. The API service says, "build ./backend", which means build a container from the Dockerfile in that folder. Same idea for front end. One file, three containers, all the wiring defined in one place.'

[24:20] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Browser window showing a "Bytemonk AI-Native Task Manager Demo" with "Create a New Task" and "Tasks" sections.
AUDIO: 'Now, create a .env file in the project root. This is where compose will read DB password from at startup. Add .env to your Git ignore file immediately before you do anything else. This file contains a password and should never go into'

[24:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'version control. Now, everything is in place. Bring the full stack up. Watch the terminal as this runs. You will see the database come up first and start its health check. The API will sit and wait. It will not start until the health check'

[24:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'passes. Then the front end comes up last, and this is exactly the startup ordering we defined with depends on. Once everything is green, let us test the API. Then the front end comes up last on HTTP localhost 3000, showing the'

[24:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'task manager interface. Here you can add new task using the add task form, mark tasks as completed by clicking the checkbox next to each task, and view updates instantly as the API syncs with'

[25:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the database. You can also perform the same operations via terminal commands. Now, let us verify the most important thing, that your data actually survived the restart. So, stop the stack and bring it back up. Your tasks are still'

[25:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'there, and that is the named volume doing its job. The database data lives completely outside the container. So, restarting or even deleting the container does not touch it. Now, let us test the other direction. What happens'

[25:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'when you actually want to wipe everything and start fresh? Gone. That is what down hyphen v does. It deletes the volumes, too, not just the containers. In development, this is exactly what you want when you need a'

[25:50] [scene:website]
VISUAL: Ollama website: "The easiest way to build with open models" with an install command.
AUDIO: 'clean slate. In production, this is the kind of command that ends careers. Know the difference and get into the habit of double-checking before you run it. If something is not behaving as expected, these three commands will tell you what'

[26:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is going on. The docker compose exec API sh command lets you drop directly into the shell inside the running service. Docker compose exec psql hyphen u task user task db also lets you drop into the'

[26:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'shell for the database service. Docker compose logs hyphen f API shows live logs from the running API container, so you can watch its output in real time. Now, this lab extends the application from lab one by adding an AI'

[26:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'service that uses a locally running LLM to generate summaries and next steps for tasks. So, instead of docker model runner, we use Ollama to run the model locally. This avoids large model downloads inside'

[26:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'docker and works better on low-resource systems. Ollama is a tool that lets us run large language models locally on our system without using any external APIs. It provides a simple API interface. So,'

[26:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'our application can generate AI responses directly from a model like 53 running on our own machine. We start by running the 53 model using Ollama. This sets up a local AI server on our system without using any external APIs.'

[27:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'On Apple silicon, this uses metal acceleration and is fast enough for development. On Nvidia hardware, it uses CUDA. On other system, it runs on CPU, which is slower, but still works for'

[27:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'development. Now, while that pulls, let us build the AI service. So, go ahead and add a new AI service folder alongside our existing back-end and front-end directories. Here\'s the AI service Dockerfile.'

[27:31] [scene:code]
VISUAL: VS Code editor showing Python code for an `ai-service` `main.py` with an `async_client.post` call to an LLM endpoint.
AUDIO: 'The core of the AI service is main.py. This service is built using FastAPI and packaged inside a Docker container. This FastAPI service provides an AI-powered summarize endpoint that generates task summaries and next'

[27:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'actions using the 53 mini model running in Ollama. We use host.docker.internal because the AI service runs inside a Docker container while Ollama runs on the host service, allowing the container'

[27:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to communicate with the local Ollama server. The endpoint validates the task title and description, creates a prompt, and sends it to Ollama through an HTTP request. The AI-generated response is then returned along with the task ID and'

[28:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'model information. The timeout of 45 seconds handle the first call, which takes longer while the model loads into memory. After that, responses come back much faster because the model stays resident. Update compose.yaml to add the AI'

[28:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'service and model provider. Bring the full stack up and test the AI endpoint. The response comes from Ollama 53 min running on your own machine. Nothing left Docker. No external API was'

[28:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'called. Now, the MCP extension. Generate a personal access token by logging into GitHub. The MCP toolkit handles the OAuth flow and stores the token as a Docker secret scope to the MCP server'

[28:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'container. Your application code never sees the raw token. Now, we need a new endpoint in the AI service that ties everything together. It calls the LLM for a summary, then passes that summary to GitHub via the'

[29:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'MCP gateway to create an issue. So, add this to main.py. The create issue endpoint first calls the summarize function to generate an AI summary of the task using the local 53'

[29:14] [scene:code]
VISUAL: VS Code editor showing `compose.yaml` with `mcp-gateway` and `ai-service` configurations, including `LLM_ENDPOINT` and `LLM_MODEL`.
AUDIO: 'model. It then connects to the MCP gateway and sends the task detail along with the AI generated summary. The MCP gateway forwards this request to GitHub, where a new issue is automatically created in the specified'

[29:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'repository. Finally, the API returns the task name, GitHub issue details, and the generated AI summary. Then, add the MCP gateway service and update the AI service environment in'

[29:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'compose.yaml. The MCP gateway service acts as a bridge between our AI application and external tools like GitHub. It runs on port 8002 and uses the GitHub token to securely authenticate API'

[29:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'requests. The AI service is configured with the MCP gateway URL, allowing it to send requests such as creating GitHub issues without directly interacting with GitHub APIs. This architecture keeps the AI service'

[30:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'simple while enabling secure access to external services through a centralized gateway. And then, restart the stack and test the full flow. With all services configured, we can now rebuild and start the complete'

[30:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'application stack. Running docker compose up --build ensures that the latest changes to the AI service and MCP gateway are included in the Docker images. We\'ll test the workflow by sending a'

[30:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'post request to the create issue endpoint. This request triggers the local 53 model to generate a summary and then uses the MCP gateway to automatically create a GitHub issue using that AI generated content.'

[30:44] [scene:outro]
VISUAL: "80% of day-to-day Docker" pie chart and "New service -> same Compose structure" text.
AUDIO: 'After executing the request, we can observe the response written by the API. The output contains the original task title, the AI generated summary, and the details of the newly created GitHub issue.'

[30:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Confirming that both the local AI processing and GitHub integrations are working successfully. That is the whole course, and I want to leave you with one thought before you go. Most people who struggle with Docker are not struggling with the commands.'

[31:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'They\'re struggling with the mental model. Once you see a container as a process with a private view of its environment, everything else starts making sense. Why data disappears when you delete a container, why localhost does not work the way you expect, why do services need'

[31:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to be on the same network to find each other? It all flows from that one idea. The commands are just how you act on that understanding. The two labs we built cover about 80% of what you\'ll actually do with Docker'

[31:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'day-to-day. The remaining 20% is just variations on the same patterns. New service, same compose structure. New language, same Dockerfile approach. New tool, same networking rules.'

[31:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And on the AI side, this space is moving fast. Docker model runner, the MCP catalog, Gemma for on Docker Hub, all of this landed in the last 6 months. If you build the habits now, you will not be'

[31:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'scrambling to catch up when these workflows become standard on every team. Take the lab project home, break it, add things to it. This is the fastest way to make any of this stick. And guys, if you found this deep dive valuable, please share it with another'

[32:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'engineer who might benefit from it. Videos like this takes us weeks of research, scripting, and production, and every share helps us more than [music] you might think. Thanks for watching, and I\'ll see you in the next one.'
